body:
### Does this issue occur when all extensions are disabled?

**No — and it cannot.** This is a bug on the extension-API surface (`vscode.window.tabGroups`), so reproducing it requires an extension to be running. The workbench UI itself is unaffected: the correct tab stays rendered and focused, and the tab bar looks right. Only the value that extensions read from `tabGroups.activeTabGroup.activeTab` is wrong.

### VS Code Version

Confirmed in the shipped desktop workbench bundles of **1.133** and **1.134** (Windows). The mechanism is still present unchanged at `main` as of `6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8`; permalinks below.

### OS Version

Windows 11 Pro 26200. Nothing in the code path is platform-specific — this should reproduce on all platforms.

### Steps to Reproduce

Run this minimal extension and invoke the `tabbug.run` command:

```ts
import * as vscode from 'vscode';

const sleep = (ms: number) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));

export function activate(context: vscode.ExtensionContext) {
  context.subscriptions.push(
    vscode.window.tabGroups.onDidChangeTabs(() => {
      console.log('activeTab =', vscode.window.tabGroups.activeTabGroup.activeTab?.label);
    })
  );

  context.subscriptions.push(
    vscode.commands.registerCommand('tabbug.run', async () => {
      // 1. Open panel A, let it become the active tab.
      const a = vscode.window.createWebviewPanel('tabbug.a', 'Panel A', vscode.ViewColumn.One, {});
      a.webview.html = '<h1>A</h1>';
      await sleep(500);

      // 2. Open panel B in the same group. B is now the active tab.
      const b = vscode.window.createWebviewPanel('tabbug.b', 'Panel B', vscode.ViewColumn.One, {});
      b.webview.html = '<h1>B</h1>';
      await sleep(500);
      console.log('before:', vscode.window.tabGroups.activeTabGroup.activeTab?.label); // "Panel B" — correct

      // 3. Change the icon of the BACKGROUND panel A. Nothing about focus or
      //    visibility changes; A stays a background tab in the workbench.
      a.iconPath = vscode.Uri.joinPath(context.extensionUri, 'media', 'icon.svg');
    })
  );
}
```

**Observed:** step 3 fires `onDidChangeTabs`, and inside the handler
`vscode.window.tabGroups.activeTabGroup.activeTab.label` is **`"Panel A"`** — a tab that is not
visible and never regained focus. It stays wrong until that tab's DTO happens to be rebuilt.

**Expected:** `activeTab.label` remains **`"Panel B"`**. Panel B is the tab actually being
rendered; step 3 only changed a background tab's icon.

Notes:

- `a.title = 'Panel A (2)'` reproduces identically — both the `iconPath` setter and the title
  setter fire the same `onDidChangeLabel` event
  ([`webviewEditorInput.ts#L138-L141`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/contrib/webviewPanel/browser/webviewEditorInput.ts#L138-L141),
  [`#L102-L106`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/contrib/webviewPanel/browser/webviewEditorInput.ts#L102-L106)).
- Webview panels are just the easiest vehicle. Any editor whose tab is updated after having once
  been active hits the same path — the dirty-indicator, pin, and preview handlers re-send the same
  cached DTO (see below).
- The `activeTab` value is repaired whenever that tab's DTO is rebuilt: opening/moving a tab, or
  anything that triggers a full `_createTabsModel()` such as adding or removing an editor group.
  That makes the symptom look intermittent and layout-dependent.

### Root cause

`isActive` is only ever computed from workbench truth in `_buildTabObject`
([`mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L94-L107`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L94-L107)),
i.e. only when a DTO is built. Afterwards, the cached DTO's `isActive` is set to `true` on
activation and **never set back to `false`**.

**1. Activation sets the flag but never clears the previous tab's**
([`mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L376-L393`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L376-L393)):

```ts
private _onDidTabActiveChange(groupId: number, editorIndex: number) {
    const tabs = this._groupLookup.get(groupId)?.tabs;
    if (!tabs) {
        return;
    }
    const activeTab = tabs[editorIndex];
    // No need to loop over as the exthost uses the most recently marked active tab
    activeTab.isActive = true;
    // Send DTO update to the exthost
    this._proxy.$acceptTabOperation({ groupId, index: editorIndex, tabDto: activeTab, kind: TabModelOperationKind.TAB_UPDATE });
}
```

The comment on L383 is the crux: skipping the loop is only safe as long as nothing else ever
re-sends one of those now-stale DTOs. Every tab visited in the session keeps `isActive: true` in
the main-thread cache.

**2. A label/icon change re-sends the stale cached DTO**
([`mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L264-L283`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L264-L283)):

```ts
private _onDidTabLabelChange(groupId: number, editorInput: EditorInput, editorIndex: number) {
    const tabId = this._generateTabId(editorInput, groupId);
    const tabInfo = this._tabInfoLookup.get(tabId);
    // If tab is found patch, else rebuild
    if (tabInfo) {
        tabInfo.tab.label = editorInput.getName();
        this._proxy.$acceptTabOperation({ groupId, index: editorIndex, tabDto: tabInfo.tab, kind: TabModelOperationKind.TAB_UPDATE });
    } else { /* ... */ }
}
```

Only `label` is patched; the rest of the cached DTO — including the stale `isActive: true` — ships
as-is. The same shape appears in
[`_onDidTabDirty` (#L401-L417)](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L401-L417),
[`_onDidTabPinChange` (#L425-L444)](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L425-L444) and
[`_onDidTabPreviewChange` (#L452-L471)](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadEditorTabs.ts#L452-L471),
so a dirty-state, pin or preview change on a previously-active background tab has the same effect.

**3. The extension host trusts `dto.isActive` on `TAB_UPDATE`**
([`extHostEditorTabs.ts#L226-L239`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostEditorTabs.ts#L226-L239)):

```ts
const tab = this._tabs.find(extHostTab => extHostTab.tabId === operation.tabDto.id);
if (!tab) {
    throw new Error('INVALID tab');
}
if (operation.tabDto.isActive) {
    this._activeTabId = operation.tabDto.id;
} else if (this._activeTabId === operation.tabDto.id && !operation.tabDto.isActive) {
    this._activeTabId = '';
}
tab.acceptDtoUpdate(operation.tabDto);
```

`this._activeTabId` is what backs both `TabGroup.activeTab`
([`#L138-L140`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostEditorTabs.ts#L138-L140))
and `Tab.isActive`
([`#L44-L47`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/6b335613aeba4f5138819b30cf87fc7cb5341dd8/src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostEditorTabs.ts#L44-L47)),
so a single stale-flag update silently repoints the whole group's notion of which tab is active.
The `else if` branch below it only fires when the incoming DTO *is* the current active tab — it
cannot defend against a *different* tab arriving with a stale `true`.

### Impact

Extensions that mirror workbench tab state get phantom active-tab changes: `activeTab` flips to a
background tab whenever that background tab's icon, title, dirty state, pin state or preview state
is updated, with no user interaction and no actual change in what is visible or focused. Because
the flag is never cleared, this affects *every* tab the user has visited during the session, and
the wrong value persists until that tab's DTO is rebuilt by unrelated activity.

The failure mode is quiet and misleading rather than loud: no exception, the workbench looks
correct, and `onDidChangeTabs` fires with a plausible-looking payload. Extensions that route work
by "which chat/document is the user currently on", mark content as seen when its tab becomes
active, or track a user-selected tab all act on a tab the user never switched to. We hit this in a
shipping extension, where it correlated tightly with background tabs updating their icons to
signal state — a pattern several editor-integrated assistants use.

### Suggested fix

In `_onDidTabActiveChange`, clear the flag on the group's other tabs before setting it on the new
one, so no stale `isActive: true` can survive in the cache:

```ts
const activeTab = tabs[editorIndex];
for (const tab of tabs) {
    tab.isActive = tab === activeTab;
}
```

That is O(tabs-in-group) on a comparatively rare event and makes the cached DTOs self-consistent,
which the update handlers already assume. It also removes the need for the defensive `else if`
branch in `acceptTabOperation`.

Alternatives, if looping is undesirable: recompute the DTO via `_buildTabObject` before re-sending
it from the update handlers, or send a partial/delta DTO on `TAB_UPDATE` so the extension host only
applies fields the event actually changed.

Happy to put up a PR for the loop version if that is the preferred direction.
